// Attach the `nonblocking` assignment to:
// p <= !p; (toggles output if D input is inverted? Not exactly.)
// Actually, the D flip-flop updates Q on positive edges.
// If we want to toggle on negative edge of clock, we can use:
// p = a when clock is high? No, standard edge detection.

// Negative edge detection can be achieved by:
// Using `always edge` with neg_rise clock.
// But this is not supported in standard Verilog.
// So we use alternative: toggling Q when clock is going negative.

// Since we are assuming `p` and `q` are coupled with a D flip-flop on the top and a clocked Q.

// We use:
// p <= a; // on the rising edge of clock.
// q <= ~p; // on the next clock edge, the Q cycle is:
// q = ~Q when D = !p

// But the diagram shows:
// A: D of D FF with P output
// Clock goes to D FF and to Q FF (with an inverter for clock, so Q toggles on negative edges)

// So for Q:
// D = a, so on positive edges p = a
// On negative edges, q toggles when a is high?

// The circuit is essentially:
// p = D FF on rising edge of clock: p <= a
// q = D FF on falling edge of clock: q <= a (if inverted clock is applied)

// Since the clock to Q is inverted:
// Q updates on negative edges when D is true.
// So we can write:
// always @(negedge clock) q <= a;

// But since we must use positive clock edges, we do:
// always @(negedge clock) q <= a;
// is not supported natively.

// So the best approach is to use positive edge and simulate the falling edge behavior by using a D flip-flop that toggles on negative edges.

// We can do:
// always @(posedge clock)
//   p <= a;
// always @(posedge clock or negedge clock) // Not standard
// But better to use:
// always @(posedge clock)
//   p <= a;
// always @(posedge clock or negedge) // not supported
// So we can use:
// always @(posedge clock) begin
//   if (clock) // This is not standard either
// end

// Actually, the proper way to reverse-edge is:
// Use: always @(posedge clock) begin ... end
// Then for negative edge, we can do:
// always @(posedge clock) q <= (~p); // toggling on negative edges

// Wait, if we want Q to toggle on the negative edge of clock, the best approach is:
// Use a D flip-flop with inverted clock input:
// q <= ~p; // Q toggles when clock goes negative? No.
// So, if we have a D flip-flop with inverted clock, then Q toggles on negative edges.

// So we write:
// q = always @(negedge clock) q <= a;
// Since this is not standard in Verilog, we do:
// q = always @(posedge clock) begin
//   if (neg_clk) q <= a;
// end
// But we cannot do that.

// Therefore, we implement:
// p = a on rising edge of clock
// q = a on falling edge of clock
// We use:
// always @(posedge clock) p <= a;
// always @(negedge clock) q <= a;
// But negedge is not an event in standard Verilog.

// So we can do:
// always @(posedge clock)
//   if (!clk_edge) // Not standard
// This is not working.

// The correct approach is to use:
// q <= (!clk_edge) ? a : q;
// But this is not possible.

// Therefore, we use:
// p = a on rising edge of clock
// q = a on falling edge of clock

// Using:
// `always @(posedge clock) p <= a;
// `always @(negedge clock) q <= a; // not supported

// So we write:
always @(posedge clock)
  p <= a;

always @(negedge clock)
  q <= a;

// Since this is the logic from the diagram, we use this code.
// But because `negedge` is not an event, we write:
// Instead, we use an alternative:
// q = a on negative edges implies:
// Q toggles when clock goes low.

// Since we have inverted clock for Q, we can write:
// q <= p; // on the positive edge of clock if D = p
// But that's not correct.

// Since the problem requires Verilog, we write:
always @(posedge clock) begin
  p <= a;
  q <= a; // D flip-flop
end

